  <h1>Hilchos Da'yonim 1</h1>
  <div class="subtitle">Laws of Judges &mdash; 1</div>
</header>

<!-- ====== OPENING VERSE BLOCK ====== -->
<div class="verse-block">
  <em>Dinay momonos bishlosha</em> &mdash; <strong>&ldquo;Monetary cases [are adjudicated] by three [judges]&rdquo;</strong>
  <span class="source-ref">Based on the teaching beginning &ldquo;<span class="aramaic">Meshra d&rsquo;sachinah b&rsquo;mai chas&rsquo;chay laih</span> etc.&rdquo; &mdash; see there, Likutay [Moharan] I, Siman 30</span>
</div>

<!-- ====== BODY TEXT ====== -->

<p>
For the <span class="key-term">da'yonim</span> <span class="key-term-def">[judges]</span> are in the aspect of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> <span class="key-term-def">[Kingship]</span>, for they rule, they summon, they compel to judgment, and they issue the verdict and the law &mdash; in the manner of a king, in the aspect of <span class="aramaic">dina d&rsquo;malchusa dina</span> [the law of the kingdom is the law]. And so too wrote Rabbainu, <em>nairoa ya&rsquo;ir</em> [may his light shine], that through <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> <span class="key-term-def">[justice/judgment]</span>, <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> ascends. And therefore the judges must be <span class="scripture">&ldquo;haters of monetary gain&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Exodus 18:21)</span>, for the rectification of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> is through [one who is a] hater of monetary gain, as Rabbainu, <em>nairoa ya&rsquo;ir</em>, wrote in the aforementioned teaching.
</p>

<p>
And this is the aspect of <span class="scripture">&ldquo;And a bribe you shall not take, for a bribe blinds the eyes of the wise&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Exodus 23:8)</span> &mdash; that is to say, through the blemish of bribery, which is the love of money, through this <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> is blemished, which is the aspect of the <em>bas ayin</em> [pupil of the eye], and it is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Chochma Tata&rsquo;a</span> <span class="key-term-def">[Lower Wisdom]</span>, see there. And this is [the precision of] &ldquo;<em>blinds</em> the eyes of the <em>wise</em>&rdquo; &mdash; <span class="precise">specifically</span>.
</p>

<p>
And therefore the judges are <strong>three</strong>, for the ascent of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> to the <span class="key-term">Ohr HaPonim</span> <span class="key-term-def">[Light of the Countenance/Face]</span>, from where it [<span class="key-term">Malchus</span>] draws its vitality, is through the <span class="key-term">Sholosh Regolim</span> <span class="key-term-def">[Three Pilgrimage Festivals]</span>, as explained there. And this is the aspect of three judges corresponding to the three festivals, through which the aforementioned <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> ascends. For the <span class="key-term">talmidai chachomim</span> <span class="key-term-def">[Torah scholars]</span> are in the aspect of <span class="key-term">Yom Tov</span> <span class="key-term-def">[a festival day]</span>, for the sanctity of <span class="key-term">Yom Tov</span> depends upon them, as Rabbainu, <em>nairoa ya&rsquo;ir</em>, wrote in Siman 135.
</p>

<p>
For the three judges who issue the verdict of law are in the aspect of <span class="scripture">&ldquo;the <em>halacha</em> follows the majority&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(as brought in the Tikkunim)</span>: <span class="aramaic">&ldquo;There is no &lsquo;majority&rsquo; less than three, for they are from the side of the Patriarchs, and regarding them it was said: the <em>halacha</em> follows the majority.&rdquo;</span> And the three festivals are in the aspect of the three <span class="key-term">Avos</span> <span class="key-term-def">[Patriarchs]</span>, as is brought. It emerges that the judges are in the aspect of the Patriarchs, the aspect of the three festivals.
</p>

<p>
And therefore they [the judges] are warned exceedingly regarding <span class="key-term">n&rsquo;si&rsquo;as ponim</span> [showing favoritism] in judgment, so as not to blemish the <span class="key-term">Ohr HaPonim</span> [Light of the Countenance], for they need to elevate <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> to the <span class="key-term">Ohr HaPonim</span> from where it draws its vitality, as written there, as above.
</p>

<p>
And this is the aspect of <span class="scripture">&ldquo;Be deliberate in judgment&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Avos 1:1)</span>. And the explanation is: that is, if a case comes before him two or three times, he should not say [&ldquo;I already ruled on this&rdquo;] etc., rather, he must investigate the law thoroughly each time. For the ascent of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> is to the <span class="key-term">Saichel</span> <span class="key-term-def">[Intellect]</span>, which is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Ohr HaPonim</span>, for it [<span class="key-term">Malchus</span>] is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Chochma Tata&rsquo;a</span> [Lower Wisdom] in which the Supernal Intellect was contracted, as explained there.
</p>

<p>
For the judges need to raise up <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> from its fall. For those who have a dispute between them &mdash; this is the aspect of the <em>fall</em> of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> that has fallen among them. For it is the statute of Kingship that people conduct themselves with integrity, and that no one commit an injustice against his fellow, but rather that there be peace and tranquility between them, as they said: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;Pray for the welfare of the kingdom, for were it not for the awe of it, a man would swallow his fellow alive&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Avos 3:2)</span>. And as was brought above, that the rectification of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> is in the aspect of peace.
</p>

<p>
And these litigants who did not conduct themselves with integrity according to the statute of Kingship, until a quarrel and judgment arose between them &mdash; it emerges that <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> has fallen among them. And therefore their obligation is to approach the judgment before the judges, and they [the judges] elevate the blemish of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> from among them, and make peace, and issue the verdict, as above.
</p>

<p>
And therefore they need each time to investigate the law with their intellect, in order to elevate <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> &mdash; for the essence of its ascent is to the Intellect, as above, from where it draws its vitality, as above. And this is [the meaning of] <span class="scripture">&ldquo;Justice, justice shall you pursue, <em>so that you may live</em>&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Deuteronomy 16:20)</span> &mdash; <span class="precise">&ldquo;so that you may live,&rdquo; specifically</span>. For through the proper <span class="key-term">bais din</span> <span class="key-term-def">[court of law]</span> that elevates <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> to the <span class="key-term">Ohr HaPonim</span> from where it receives its vitality, as above &mdash; through this, vitality is drawn down below as well, for all of them receive vitality through it [<span class="key-term">Malchus</span>].
</p>

<p>
And this is what our Sages, of blessed memory, said: <span class="aramaic">&ldquo;From the court, [even if they] took his cloak, he sings and goes on his way&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Sanhedrin 7a)</span>. For it is brought there in the aforementioned teaching, at its conclusion, that the rectification of the <span class="key-term">kol</span> <span class="key-term-def">[voice]</span> is through the aspect of the rectification of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>, see there. And therefore, after he has accepted the judgment &mdash; at that point the aspect of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> has been rectified for him, which was previously blemished for him, as above. And therefore &ldquo;he sings and goes on his way&rdquo; etc., for the aspect of the voice has been rectified, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>, as above.
</p>

