<!-- ====== TRANSLATOR SUMMARY ====== -->
<div class="translator-summary">
  <h3>Summary &mdash; From the Translator</h3>

  <p>
  This teaching connects the laws of the three-judge court to the Kabbalistic structure of <em>Malchus</em> (Kingship/Sovereignty). The judges correspond to the three Pilgrimage Festivals (<em>Sholosh Regolim</em>), which in turn correspond to the three Patriarchs. Their role is to elevate <em>Malchus</em> &mdash; which has "fallen" when people dispute &mdash; back up to the <em>Ohr HaPonim</em> (Light of the Countenance), the Supernal Intellect, from which <em>Malchus</em> draws its vitality.
  </p>

  <p>
  Key points: (a) Judges are in the aspect of Kingship because they rule and enforce law. (b) Through proper justice, <em>Malchus</em> ascends. (c) Bribery blemishes <em>Malchus</em>, which is identified with <em>Chochma Tata&rsquo;a</em> (Lower Wisdom) and the "pupil of the eye" &mdash; hence, a bribe literally "blinds." (d) The three judges correspond to the three festivals, the three Patriarchs, and the principle of "the <em>halacha</em> follows the majority." (e) Dispute between people is itself a fall of <em>Malchus</em>, and the court process repairs it. (f) "Justice, justice shall you pursue <em>so that you may live</em>" &mdash; the vitality drawn down through proper judgment sustains all. (g) One who accepts the court's verdict, even losing his cloak, "sings on his way" &mdash; because the voice (<em>kol</em>), which is an aspect of <em>Malchus</em>, has been rectified.
  </p>

  <p class="translator-note">
    &mdash; Translator&rsquo;s summary for ajew.org
  </p>
</div>

